index 0000000..c501dc6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/dicode-codex-mvp.md
@@ -0,0 +1,72 @@
+# DiCode Codex MVP deployment
+
+The standalone `dicode-codex-bridge` receives `POST /codex/v1/responses`. It checks the caller's DiCode bearer token and selected model through the existing `model-proxy` model-access service. After that service validates the JWT, the bridge reads its email claim, looks up `chat_rag:new_token_auth` in the existing Redis, and on a miss calls the same email-to-key service as chat-rag. It sends the returned per-user key to the Responses gateway, with `originator: codex_cli_rs` and session/thread IDs, and streams the original events. The VS Code extension starts an unmodified local Codex CLI; no Codex process runs in the Kubernetes container.
+
+## Offline artifacts
+
+The bridge logging release contains `dicode-codex-bridge-v0.1.3-linux-amd64-image.tar` and a SHA-256 file. The earlier DiCode Codex MVP release contains the CLI asset archive and VSIX. The bridge image is for **linux/amd64** cluster nodes.
+
+## Install the new service only
+
+1. On a machine with Docker and access to your internal registry, run:
+
+   ```sh
+   docker load -i dicode-codex-bridge-v0.1.3-linux-amd64-image.tar
+   docker tag costrict/dicode-codex-bridge:v0.1.3 REGISTRY/PROJECT/dicode-codex-bridge:v0.1.3
+   docker push REGISTRY/PROJECT/dicode-codex-bridge:v0.1.3
+   ```
+
+2. Create a Secret containing the **token exchange service's `NEW-OPEN-TOKEN` credential**. Use the same value as `newTokenAuth.openToken` in the current chat-rag configuration; this is not a gateway API key. Do not put it in `configure.sh` or the VSIX:
+
+   ```sh
+   kubectl -n costrict create secret generic dicode-codex-token-exchange \
+     --from-file=open-token=/secure/path/new-open-token.txt \
+     --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
+   ```
+
+3. In this deployment repository, set `IMAGE_DICODE_CODEX_BRIDGE` in `scripts/newest-images.list` to `REGISTRY/PROJECT/dicode-codex-bridge:v0.1.3`. Confirm `CODEX_GATEWAY_RESPONSES_URL` points to the gateway's exact `/v1/responses` endpoint and `CODEX_TOKEN_EXCHANGE_URL` matches chat-rag's current `newTokenAuth.endpoint`. The caller's DiCode JWT is used for model authorization; the token exchange service uses its own Secret credential, and the gateway uses the resulting user's key. The bridge reads the existing `redis:6379` service and `chat_rag:new_token_auth` cache.
+4. Run `./costrict.sh prepare`. Check the generated `k8s/dicode-codex-bridge.yaml`, then apply only this new service:
+
+   ```sh
+   kubectl apply -f k8s/dicode-codex-bridge.yaml
+   kubectl -n costrict rollout status deployment/dicode-codex-bridge
+   ```
+
+5. Set the APISIX route. In one terminal run `kubectl -n costrict port-forward svc/apisix 39180:9180`. In another terminal, from this repository:
+
+   ```sh
+   source ./configure.sh
+   export APISIX_ADDR="127.0.0.1:39180"
+   export AUTH="X-API-KEY: ${APIKEY_APISIX_ADMIN}"
+   export TYPE="Content-Type: application/json"
+   ./scripts/apisix_router/dicode-codex-bridge.sh
+   ```
+
+   The public route is `POST /codex/v1/responses`. You can also use the existing `./apisix_router_setting.sh` workflow, which reapplies all routes.
+
+For a direct check, `kubectl -n costrict port-forward svc/dicode-codex-bridge 18080:8080`, then send a Responses request to `http://127.0.0.1:18080/codex/v1/responses` using a **DiCode user bearer token** and a model visible in `/ai-gateway/api/v1/models`. Do not send a gateway key to the bridge as the user token. Check `kubectl -n costrict logs deployment/dicode-codex-bridge` if the model check, Redis lookup, email-to-key exchange, or gateway call fails.
+
+## View bridge logs in Grafana
+
+The existing Promtail DaemonSet scrapes all Pod stdout/stderr in the CoStrict namespace and attaches the Pod's `app` label. The bridge Deployment has `app: dicode-codex-bridge`, and Grafana already has a Loki data source. No new collector or data source is needed. Provision the dashboard after updating this repository:
+
+```sh
+kubectl -n costrict create configmap costrict-grafana-dashboards \
+  --from-file=apisix-grafana-dashboard.json=config/grafana/dashboards/apisix-grafana-dashboard.json \
+  --from-file=dicode-codex-bridge-logs.json=config/grafana/dashboards/dicode-codex-bridge-logs.json \
+  --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
+kubectl -n costrict rollout restart deployment/grafana
+kubectl -n costrict rollout status deployment/grafana
+```
+
+Open **DiCode Codex Bridge Logs** in Grafana, or use Loki Explore with `{app="dicode-codex-bridge"}`. The upper panel includes request summaries and every upstream HTTP status. The lower panel filters `gateway_error:` and `gateway_stream_error:`. Request logs include model, tool names, and instruction digests but omit tokens and user text. Gateway error logs include bounded error messages from HTTP errors and SSE error events. These logs are always enabled.
+
+## Codex CLI auto-install assets
+
+With a running `portal` deployment, execute `./scripts/deploy-dicode-codex-cli-assets.sh /path/to/dicode-codex-cli-0.164.0-assets.tar`. This places the platform manifests and archives under `/var/www/costrict/codex/` on the existing portal volume. Verify from a workstation that `<APISIX_BASE_URL>/costrict/codex/linux/x64/manifest.json` and `<APISIX_BASE_URL>/costrict/codex/win32/x64/manifest.json` return JSON. The VSIX downloads the matching archive on first activation, checks its SHA-256, and installs it in the extension host's private storage. For later CLI releases, create a new asset archive using `node scripts/package-dicode-codex-cli.js VERSION LINUX_X64_TGZ WINDOWS_X64_TGZ LINUX_ARM64_TGZ WINDOWS_ARM64_TGZ OUTPUT_TAR` from the main repository, then redeploy these static assets.
+
+## Workstation
+
+Install the separate `dicode-codex-mvp.vsix` on the workstation or remote extension host. The VSIX automatically downloads and installs Codex CLI on first activation when `dicodeCodex.codexPath` is left at its default `codex`. Set `dicodeCodex.serverUrl` to the APISIX entry URL if the default is not your internal address. An explicit `dicodeCodex.codexPath` overrides automatic installation. On Windows, the extension host downloads the Windows package; on WSL or remote Linux, it downloads the Linux package.
+
+The bridge's `/health` endpoint is only for Kubernetes probes. Model authorization is checked on every Responses request. Existing login, model list, and Chat-RAG routes remain on their current services.
diff --git a/k8s/dicode-codex-bridge.yaml.tpl b/k8s/dicode-codex-bridge.yaml.tpl
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9df2b6e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/k8s/dicode-codex-bridge.yaml.tpl
@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+apiVersion: v1
+kind: Service
+metadata:
+  name: dicode-codex-bridge
+  namespace: {{K8S_NAMESPACE}}
+spec:
+  selector:
+    app: dicode-codex-bridge
+  ports:
+    - name: http
+      port: 8080
+      targetPort: 8080
+---
+apiVersion: apps/v1
+kind: Deployment
+metadata:
+  name: dicode-codex-bridge
+  namespace: {{K8S_NAMESPACE}}
+spec:
+  replicas: 1
+  selector:
+    matchLabels:
+      app: dicode-codex-bridge
+  template:
+    metadata:
+      labels:
+        app: dicode-codex-bridge
+    spec:
+      nodeSelector:
+        {{K8S_NODE_SELECTOR_KEY}}: "{{K8S_NODE_SELECTOR_VALUE}}"
+      containers:
+        - name: bridge
+          image: {{IMAGE_DICODE_CODEX_BRIDGE}}
+          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
+          env:
+            - name: MODEL_ACCESS_URL
+              value: "http://model-proxy:8080/ai-gateway/api/v1/models"
+            - name: GATEWAY_RESPONSES_URL
+              value: "{{CODEX_GATEWAY_RESPONSES_URL}}"
+            - name: TOKEN_EXCHANGE_URL
+              value: "{{CODEX_TOKEN_EXCHANGE_URL}}"
+            - name: REDIS_ADDR
+              value: "redis:6379"
+            - name: TOKEN_EXCHANGE_OPEN_TOKEN
+              valueFrom:
+                secretKeyRef:
+                  name: dicode-codex-token-exchange
+                  key: open-token
+          ports:
+            - name: http
+              containerPort: 8080
+          readinessProbe:
+            httpGet:
+              path: /health
+              port: http
+          livenessProbe:
+            httpGet:
+              path: /health
+              port: http
